As expected, mobile weapon overlaps actions (weapon x245..375 y759..824, fire x288..372 y694..778, slide below? actions y670..824). Momentum overlaps stick (x16..190 y731..824; stick x20..144 y700..824). Need fix.

Quick tap fired 2 shots because fireHeld for 1ms? It shouldn't; at low FPS maybe two physics steps with same firePressed plus `fireHeld` for 1ms ended before next frame. firePressed true and cooldown; why 2? Wait after fire(1), setTimeout false after 1ms. The next frame simulation runs *all catch-up steps* 16; input.firePressed stays true throughout the frame; the loop steps 16, cooldown .088, so after 0.088 game seconds maybe another shot. Since `wantsFire` includes firePressed, it remains true for all substeps in the frame, so multiple shots fire within one low-FPS frame. This means a single click could fire 2-3 bullets at low FPS. We need latch firePressed as a single shot, not allow it across multiple fixed steps. The input.pressed set is cleared at endFrame (after all steps), so one-shot intent persists across simulation catch-up. For jump, this is okay (grounded check prevents multiple jumps? jumpBuffer would be set multiple times but after first jump canJump false). For fire, `wantsFire` needs to be true only for the first step.

Approach: Add a consumed flag to input or in combat:
```
if (input.firePressed && !this._firedOnPress) { ... }
```
But firePressed stays true all frame; we can consume by modifying `input.firePressed = false` after one shot. But then auto reload empty click handling? If we set false after shoot, fine. However main's `_firing` update uses input.firePressed after simulation; it would be false. Could set player._firing from fireHeld only; quick tap not visible. Fine.

In Combat.update:
```
const pressed = input.firePressed;
if (this.enabled && (input.fireHeld || pressed) && ... ) {
  this._shoot();
  if (pressed) input.firePressed = false; // consume so catch-up steps don't fire again
}
```
But auto-fire while holding still fires at cadence. If user taps, one shot per mouse press; good. If input.firePressed is set again by a new mousedown next frame, fires. FireHeld might still be true after quick tap? Input mouseup sets fireHeld false. If the click is shorter than a frame, fireHeld false by sim; only pressed. Good.
When holding, first frame after mousedown: pressed true, fireHeld true. We shoot, then set pressed false. Subsequent steps fire via fireHeld. Good.
Need be careful that `input.firePressed` is also used for empty click auto reload. If ammo 0 and pressed, previous code starts reload. If we clear pressed after `_shoot`, and ammo becomes 0 -> `_startReload`, fine. If not shoot due to cooldown/reloading, pressed remains until endFrame; on next frames? It may retry. If cooldown >0 but pressed true, no shot and pressed remains; in catch-up steps after cooldown expires, it would shoot even though click occurred earlier. That's acceptable (input buffered) but could double-fire. Better treat firePressed as a buffered shot only if cooldown allows. We can consume on any frame where `wantsFire`, even if cooldown>0? That might drop a quick click that occurs during cooldown. But mousedown during automatic fire is common; fireHeld covers it. For a click during cooldown, one shot is queued and should fire when available? Could process. But to avoid multiple shots in a burst frame, we can add a `this._queuedShot` flag:
```
if (input.firePressed) this._queuedShot = true;
...
if (enabled && (fireHeld || queuedShot) && !reloading && ammo>0 && cooldown<=0) {
  this._queuedShot = false;
  _shoot();
}
```
But if firePressed remains true for subsequent steps, queuedShot gets set true again every step. We need consume input.firePressed immediately after reading. Options:
```
const pressed = input.firePressed;
if (pressed) input.firePressed = false;
if (pressed) this._queuedShot = true;
if (this._queuedShot && ready) { _queuedShot=false; _shoot(); }
```
This moves a click into internal queue and clears the input latch. If click occurs during cooldown, queue holds until ready. Auto reload etc. Good. But clearing input.firePressed in combat means main _firing uses it after simulation and gets false. Not important.
- However if combat disabled at click time (e.g. menu?) input not enabled. Fine.
- Need reset `_queuedShot` in reset.
- Empty click: if ammo<=0 and pressed, start reload. We cleared firePressed, so check a local `pressed` variable. Use local for both.
- If clicked during reload and ammo 0? queue may persist and auto fire after reload; maybe weird. We can clear queue on reload. Or avoid queueing while reloading. Simpler:
```
const pressed = input.firePressed;
if (pressed) input.firePressed = false;
if (this.enabled && pressed && this.ammo <=0 && !reloading) { auto reload; }
const wantsFire = input.fireHeld || (pressed && !this.reloading && this.ammo>0);
if (ready) _shoot();
```
This fires at most once from card pressed in this update step, but if cooldown >0, click lost. At 680 rpm, clicks within cooldown are rare except spam; losing a click during cooldown is normal in FPS. Holding fire is the norm. Could avoid by a queued shot boolean but that's more complexity.
- A pressed click could also fire in the same update as fireHeld; after first shot, catch-up steps auto fire via fireHeld if click actually held. That's normal: if fireHeld true (mouse still down), auto fire, so a 1ms click where mouseup already happened has fireHeld false and only one shot. Good.
- But for a click that happens while fireHeld true? FireHeld already true from earlier, auto fire; pressing again no effect. fine.
- So use local pressed and clear `input.firePressed = false` immediately when combat consumes it. In low FPS, this prevents repeated "pressed" shots within catch-up. But if the mouse is still held, fireHeld causes auto fire, as intended. The test `fire(1)` sets fireHeld false after 1ms, likely before next frame, so one shot. Great.

Potential conflict: main._firing uses input.firePressed after simulation but it will be false. If quick tapping one shot, HUD is-firing won't pulse. No big deal. We could track a cooldown to show but no.

Alternatively input.firePressed reset in first physics step? No, for jump preservation. Combats only.

Also `input.firePressed` is used in main? no. So okay.

Need test `.fire(ms)` for debug. With ms=80, fireHeld likely true for the first frame, causing two shots in catch-up. That's expected. For precise single tests use ms=1.

Wait our quick tap test called fire(1) but got 2 shots because firePressed wasn't consumed. With fix one shot.

- Respawn before/after both false because target 0 wasn't killed in audit. No issue; combat test earlier killed and then no respawn check. We'll test respawn separately by forcing target.down or shooting. Could use `target.hit(200)`, then wait 3s. Do later.

Mobile layout needs fix. Let's inspect screenshots.

